  "body": " Set Theoretic Foundations  Rigorous mathematics needs some starting place. As a community, we have generally decided the best foundations and language is that of sets. We will take such a set-theoretic approach, which means we will base our notions on underlying concepts and notation from naive set theory.   Sets   A sets is a collection of objects.    Think of sets as containers.   Elements   If is a set, then an object within is an element of . We denote this .     Subset   A subset of a set is a collection of objects contained within . We denote this . In other words, if implies .    The empty set  is the set with no elements. The empty set is a subset of all sets.   Equality of Sets   Two set and are equal they have they both have the same collection of elements.    You should be familiar with the following standard set notation :   should be read and understood as: \" is the set of all objects satisfying condition .\"   You should have a basic understanding of the following fundamental set operations.   Set Operations   Let and be sets.     Intersection , denoted , is the set of all elements in  and  . In symbols,      Union , denoted , is the set of all elements in  or  . In symbols,      Difference , denoted or , is the set of all elements in  but not  . In symbols,      Cartesian product , denoted , is the set of pairs of elements in and . In symbols,        Set Map   If and are sets, a set map  is a rule that assigns to each element of an element of . We call the domain of and the codomain (or target ) of .     Equality of Set Maps   Two set maps and are equal they do the same thing to each input. In symbols, for all , . We denote this .    "
