  "title": "Uniqueness of Identities and Inverses",
  "body": " Uniqueness of Identities and Inverses  In this investigation, you now revisit basic algebraic properties of , properties you may have taken for granted, but you cannot do so for its abstraction. You will practice deducing these basis properties from field axioms.  In we assume identity elements and inverses exist , we do not assume they are unique.   Additive identities are unique   If is a field and both satisfy the property that, for all , and , then .     Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum . You may find field property (F4) in useful.    Multiplicative identities are unique   If is a field and both satisfy the property that, for all , and , then .     Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?   We are therefore justified in discussing the identity element.   Additive inverses are unique   If is a field, , and satisfy the property that and , then .     Try a direct proof. Analyze the sum in two ways. Remember, addition is a binary operation, so which pair do you add first? You may find field properties (F1), (F2) in useful.    Multiplicative inverses are unique   If is a field, , and satisfy the property that and , then .     Have you seen a similar result whose proof you may leverage?   We are therefore justified in discussing the inverse. Going forward, we will use the notation to denote the additive inverse of and to denote the multiplicative inverse of .  "
