  "title": "Definition",
  "body": " Definition  In your first course in linear algebra, you worked with , length lists of real numbers. We called such lists vectors. We were able to mix vectors, or more precisely, add and scale vectors. These mixing properties allowed us to construct the concepts and tools of matrix algebra over : matrix equations, bases, decompositions, eigentheory, and more.  We return to in the context of linear algebra, to perform structural abstraction and give life to an (abstract) vector space over a field.  (I am trusting that you are comfortable with the concepts and tools from working with in your first course in linear algebra. This familiarity provides motivation, intuition, examples, and more generally, a robust concept image for what follows. I encourage you to consult my Concepts of Linear Algebra (COLA) text. Engage with the exposition, play with the GeoGebra manipulatives, and work out the homeworks.)   Vector Space   If is a field, then a vector space over is a set with two maps which we call addition ( ) and scaling  :   satisfying:    Addition is associative : If , then .    Addition has an identity (which we call a zero vector ): There exists an element such that if , then .    Addition has inverses : If , then there exists and element such that .    Addition is commutative : If , then .    Scaling is associative : If and , then .    Scaling has an identity : If is the multiplicative identity and , then .     Distributivity of scaling 1: If and , then .     Distributivity of scaling 2: If and , then .    Elements of a vector space are called vectors . Elements of are called scalars .    To help distinguish vectors from scalars, when writing by hand, I will put an arrow (e.g. ) over each vector, and when ing, I will always use \\vect{} for each vector. When is clear from context, I will write instead of .   Linear Combination   If , then a linear combination of is an expression of the form   where .  The trivial linear combination is the one with all 0 scalars. Otherwise it is nontrivial .    Note that we only take finite sums in linear combinations.   Addition is a binary operation , meaning it has two inputs. What does an expression like even mean?   "
