  "body": " Span  One core idea of linear algebra is mixture . Vector space axioms were precisely the structures necessary to allow for mixtures, which we describe formally as linear combinations.  Throughout this investigation, will denote a vector space over the field .   Span   The span of , denoted , is the set of all linear combinations of vectors in . (Note that can be infinite, but the combinations are finite.) In symbols,   We define the span of the empty set to be the trivial subspace.     Span of contains   If is nonempty, then .     Try a direct proof. Allow the definitions to structure your proof. You may find and useful.    Span is a subspace   If is nonempty, then is a vector subspace.     Try a direct proof. Allow the definitions to structure your proof. To show (S1), you will need to show that, if , then . To do this, you need to interpret and unravel definitions. Similar for (S2). You may find , , , , and useful.     If is a subspace and , then .     Try a direct proof. You need to show that any linear combination of vectors in is contained within . You may find , , , and useful.    Spanning Set   If is a subspace and such that , then we say is a spanning set for , or equivalently,  spans  .    Please note how span is used in two ways: span , the noun, denotes the set of mixtures of a set of vectors; meanwhile spans , the verb, is a (true\/false) relationship between a set of vectors and a subspace .  A useful alternative characterization of span is that it is the smallest subspace containing , which is made precise by the following proposition.   Span( ) is the smallest subspace containing   If is nonempty, then is equal to the intersection of all subspaces of which contain .     Try a direct proof. Let denote the collection of all subspaces of containing . In symbols, . Then you want to show . You will need to show both inclusions. To show that is immediate (why???). To show the other inclusion, observe is in each for which , and deduce (how???). You may find , , and useful.   "
